          %h4#for_linear_group_representations For linear group representations
          %p
            Still for the special case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            and let the algebra in question specifically be its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = k[G]
            \. Then the category of linear
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Rep(G) \simeq k[G] Mod
          %p and we obtain
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p
              %strong (Tannaka duality for linear group representations)
            %p There is a natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Rep
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    End(Rep(G) \to Vect) \simeq k[G]
                    \\,.
          %p
            This should work for group rings as well, say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \mathbf{Z}[G].
            However, note that the group ring does not recover the group in general (cf
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1203202/recovering-a-group-from-its-group-algebra-over-mathbbz"} discussion 1
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/324391/is-it-possible-to-reconstruct-a-finitely-generated-group-from-its-category-of-re"} discussion 2
            and papers referred therein).
          %h4#Coalgebras For coalgebra comodules
          %p
            If for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            we choose not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            but its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect^{op}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : A Mod \to Vect
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %em An introduction to Tannaka duality and quantum groups
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mq.edu.au/~street/CT90Como.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "http://www.maths.mq.edu.au/~street/CT90Como.pdf#page=40"}> proposition 5, page 40
            )
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat%C3%A9gories+Tannakiennes"} Catégories Tannakiennes
          %p
            it is shown that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is recovered as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi fin
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int^{N \in A Mod_{fin}} F(N) \otimes F(N)^*
          %p
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            itself is finite dimensional then this is yet again just a special case of the enriched Yoneda lemma for
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi FinDimVect
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = FinDimVect^{op}
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite"} opposite
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is recovered as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi fin
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A = \int_{N \in A Mod_{fin}} V(F(N), F(N))
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect^{op}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots \simeq \int^{N \in A Mod}( Vect(F(N), F(N)))
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDimVect(V,W) \simeq V\otimes W^*
            the above coend expression follows.
          %p
            As before, more work is required to show that even for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            itself not finite dimensional, it is still recovered in terms of the above (co)end over just its finite dimensional modules.
          %h3#for_lie_groupoids For Lie groupoids
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality+for+Lie+groupoids"} Tannaka duality for Lie groupoids
          %h3#for_geometric_stacks For geometric stacks
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality+for+geometric+stacks"} Tannaka duality for geometric stacks
          %h3#InHigherCategoryTheory In higher category theory
          %p
            In as far as the proof of Tannaka duality only depends on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            whenever a higher generalization of the Yoneda lemma is available.
          %p
            This is notably the case for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            theory, where we have the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Yoneda+lemma"} (∞,1)-Yoneda lemma
          %h4#ForInfinityPermutations
            For permutation
            = succeed "-representations" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            By applying the
            = succeed "-Yoneda" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ForPermutationRepresentations"} for permutation representations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-permutation+representations"} ∞-permutation representations
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (Tannaka duality for
                = succeed "-permutation" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Rep
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep_{\infty Grpd}(G) := Func(\mathbf{B}G, \infty Grpd)
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-permutation+representations"} ∞-permutation representations
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-functors
              from its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Rep
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F :  Rep_{\infty Grpd}(G) \to \infty Grpd
              be the fiber functor that remembers the underlying
              = succeed "-groupoid." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+a+quasi-category"} equivalence in a quasi-category
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Rep
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    End(Rep_{\infty Grpd}(G) \to \infty Grp) \simeq G
                    \\,.
          %p
            As before, this holds immediately even for representations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (Tannaka duality for
                = succeed "-permutation" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Rep
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mi Func
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep_{\infty Grpd}(C) := Func(C,\infty Grpd)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Rep
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_c : Rep_{\infty Grpd}(C) \to \infty Grpd
              for the corresponding fiber functor.
            %p Then there is a natural equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hom(F_c, F_{c'}) \simeq C(c,c')
            %p
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %h4#InfinityGaloisTheory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-Galois theory
          %p
            As a special case of this, we obtain a statement about
            = succeed "-Galois" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} homotopy groups in an (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+contractible+space"} locally contractible space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LConst
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LConst(X)
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+%E2%88%9E-stack"} locally constant ∞-stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Rep
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep_{\infty Grpd}(\Pi(X))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            a point, write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi LConst
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_x : LConst(X) \to \infty Grpd
            for the corresponding fiber functor.
          %p Then we have
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_7 Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              there is a natural
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LConst
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi x
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi Aut
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    End(LConst(X) \stackrel{F_x}{\to} \infty Grpd) \simeq
                    \\mathbf{B} Aut_{\Pi(X)}(x)
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular we have
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural isomorphisms
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mi End
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LConst
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi x
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_n End(LConst(X) \stackrel{F_x}{\to} \infty Grpd)
                    \\simeq
                    \\pi_n(X,x)
                    \\,.
          %p
            More on this is at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+--+structures"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos – structures
            in the section
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+--+structures#Homotopy"} Galois theory in a cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannakian+category"} Tannakian category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne%27s+theorem+on+tensor+categories"} Deligne's theorem on tensor categories
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An introduction to Tannaka duality and quantum groups
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mq.edu.au/~street/CT90Como.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                B.J. Day,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Enriched Tannaka reconstruction
                %strong 108
                (1996) 17-22,
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(95)00039-9"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat%C3%A9gories+Tannakiennes"} Catégories Tannakiennes
          %p The following paper shortens Deligne’s proof
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Rosenberg"}> Alexander L. Rosenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The existence of fiber functors
          %p Deligne’s proof in turn fills the gap in the seminal work with the same title
          %ul
            %li
              N. Saavedra Rivano, “Catégories Tannakiennes.”
              %em Bulletin de la Société Mathématique de France
              100 (1972): 417-430.
              %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/87193"} EuDML
          %p A revival in algebraic geometry related to the theory of mixed motives was marked by
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/P.+Deligne"}> P. Deligne
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+Milne"} J. Milne
              = succeed "," do
                %em Tannakian categories
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 900
              %a{:href => "http://jmilne.org/math/xnotes/tc.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Analogous discussion for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} stable (infinity,1)-categories
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Wallbridge12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Wallbridge"}> James Wallbridge
                ,
                %em Tannaka duality over ring spectra
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5787"}> arXiv:1204.5787
                )
            %li#Iwanari14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isamu+Iwanari"}> Isamu Iwanari
                ,
                %em Tannaka duality and stable infinity-categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1409.3321"}> arXiv:1409.3321
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
          %p Ulbrich made a major contribution at the coalgebra and Hopf algebra level
          %ul
            %li
              K-H. Ulbrich,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On Hopf algebras and rigid monoidal categories
              %strong 72
              (1990), no. 1-2, 252–256,
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF02764622"} doi
          %p This Hopf-direction has been advanced by many authors including
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S.+L.+Woronowicz"}> S. L. Woronowicz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Tannaka-Krein duality for compact matrix pseudogroups. Twisted
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SU
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(N)
                    groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 93
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01393687"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shahn+Majid"}> Shahn Majid
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations of quantum group theory
            %li
              %p
                Phung Ho Hai,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tannaka-Krein duality for Hopf algebroids
                %strong 167
                (1):193–225 (2008)
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0206113"} math.QA/0206113
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Volodymyr+Lyubashenko"}> Volodymyr V. Lyubashenko
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modular transformations and tensor categories
                %strong 98
                (1995) 279–327
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(94)00045-K"} doi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Squared Hopf algebras and reconstruction theorems
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 40
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9605035"} q-alg/9605035
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Squared Hopf algebras
                %strong 142
                (677):x 180, 1999; Алгебры Хопфа и вектор-симметрии, УМН, 41:5(251) (1986), 185–186,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=rm&paperid=2247&what=fullt&option_lang=rus"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hopf algebras and vector symmetries
            %li
              %p
                A. Bruguières,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Théorie tannakienne non commutative
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 22
            %li
              %p
                K. Szlachanyi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fiber functors, monoidal sites and Tannaka duality for bialgebroids
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1578"} arxiv/0907.1578
            %li
              %p
                B. Day, R. Street,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum categories, star autonomy, and quantum groupoids
                %strong 43
                (2004) 187-225
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Sch%C3%A4ppi"}> Daniel Schäppi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The formal theory of Tannaka duality
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.5213"} arxiv/1112.5213
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tannaka duality for comonoids in cosmoi
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.0977"} arXiv:0911.0977
          %p
            A generalization of several classical reconstruction theorems with nontrivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Rosenberg"}> Alexander L. Rosenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reconstruction+of+Groups"} Reconstruction of groups
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 9
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s00029-003-0322-x"} doi
          %p
            Categorically oriented notes were written also by Pareigis, emphasising on using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} Coend
            in dual picture. His works can be found
            %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~pareigis/pa_schft.html"} here
            but the most important is the chapter 3 of his online book
          %ul
            %li
              Bodo Pareigis,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum groups and noncommutative geometry
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~pareigis/Vorlesungen/98SS/Quantum_Groups/LN3.PDF"} pdf
          %p A very neat Tannaka theorem for stacks is proved in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tannaka duality for geometric stacks
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412266"} arXiv:math.AG/0412266
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quasi-Coherent+Sheaves+and+Tannaka+Duality+Theorems"} Quasi-Coherent Sheaves and Tannaka Duality Theorems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"}> Bertrand Toen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.msri.org/publications/ln/msri/2002/hodgetheory/toen/2/index.html"} Higher Tannaka duality
            %li
              %p
                Moshe Kamensky,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Model theory and the Tannakian formalism
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0604"} arXiv:0908.0604
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tannakian formalism over fields with operators
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.7285"} arxiv/1111.7285
            %li
              %p
                H. Fukuyama, I. Iwanari,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monoidal infinity category of complexes from Tannakian viewpoint
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1004.3087"} arxiv/1004.3087
            %li
              %p
                remark of
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/3446/tannakian-formalism/3467#3467"} Ben-Zvi on Tannaka reconstruction for monoidal categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Kazhdan"}> David Kazhdan
                , Michael Larsen, Yakov Varshavsky,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Tannakian formalism and the Langlands conjectures
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1006.3864"} arxiv/1006.3864
          %p The classical articles are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tadao+Tannaka"}> Tadao Tannaka
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Über den Dualitätssatz der nichtkommutativen topologischen Gruppen
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka-Krein+theorem"} Tannaka-Krein theorem
            %li
              %p
                N. Tatsuuma,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A duality theorem for locally compact groups
            %li
              %p
                Nobuhiko Tatsuuma,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Duality theorem for locally compact groups and some related topics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+G.+Krein"}> Mark G. Krein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A principle of duality for bicompact groups and quadratic block algebras
                %strong 69
                (1949), 725–728.
            %li
              %p
                Eiichi Abe,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dualité de Tannaka des groupes algébriques
          %p
            The Tannaka-type reconstruction in quantum field theory see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
          %p
            Tannaka duality in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Wallbridge"}> James Wallbridge
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher Tannaka duality
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://digital.library.adelaide.edu.au/dspace/bitstream/2440/69436/1/02whole.pdf"} Adelaide University repository
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5787"} arXiv:1204.5787
          %p Tannaka duality for dg-categories is studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J.P.Pridham,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tannaka duality for enhanced triangulated categories
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1309.0637"} arxiv/1309.0637
            %li
              %p
                MathOverflow,
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/30453/does-the-tannaka-krein-theorem-come-from-an-equivalence-of-2-categories"} Does the Tannaka-Krein theorem come from an equivalence of 2-categories?
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Lukas Rollier.
              %em Equivariant Tannaka-Krein reconstruction and quantum automorphism groups of discrete structures
              (2024). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03364"}> arXiv:2405.03364
              ).
